@page "/tradeoffs"

<h2>Trade-offs</h2>

<section class="topology-grid">
    <article class="card">
        <h3>Microservices</h3>
        <h4>Pros</h4>
        <ul>
            <li>Independent deployment boundaries.</li>
            <li>Clear ownership by business capability.</li>
            <li>Different services can scale independently.</li>
            <li>Explicit API contracts between services.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Cons</h4>
        <ul>
            <li>More deployable units and runtime processes.</li>
            <li>More service-to-service network calls.</li>
            <li>Compensation and idempotency must be explicit.</li>
            <li>Local development and operations have more moving parts.</li>
        </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="card">
        <h3>Virtual actors</h3>
        <h4>Pros</h4>
        <ul>
            <li>Natural fit for many stateful identities.</li>
            <li>Per-identity coordination is localized.</li>
            <li>Inventory reservations are serialized by product identity.</li>
            <li>Workflow code can be easier to follow when state belongs to an actor.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Cons</h4>
        <ul>
            <li>The Orleans runtime becomes part of the operational model.</li>
            <li>Hot grains can become bottlenecks.</li>
            <li>Persistence, placement, and clustering choices matter.</li>
            <li>Deployment independence is different from microservice boundaries.</li>
        </ul>
    </article>
</section>

<section class="card callout">
    <h3>Concurrency note</h3>
    <p>The concurrent orders scenario is not intended to show that microservices are inherently unsafe. Both implementations are expected to prevent over-reservation.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Microservices:</strong> Inventory.Api owns inventory state and must explicitly protect the reservation invariant.</li>
        <li><strong>Virtual actors:</strong> InventoryItemGrain owns product inventory state and serializes calls for a product identity.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The important difference is where the concurrency rule lives. Microservices require explicit concurrency control at the state owner. Virtual actors make per-identity serialization a natural part of the programming model.</p>
</section>

<section class="card callout">
    <h3>Performance note</h3>
    <p>Elapsed time in the dashboard is useful for local feedback, but it is not a benchmark.</p>
    <p>The microservice path crosses more HTTP service boundaries in this sample. The virtual actor path keeps more workflow coordination inside the Orleans runtime path. Production performance depends on persistence, network topology, placement, hot keys, deployment shape, and operational tuning.</p>
</section>

<section class="card callout">
    <h3>Practical takeaway</h3>
    <p>Microservices are useful when the system boundary is deployable, organizational, and capability-oriented.</p>
    <p>Virtual actors are useful when the hard part is coordinating many stateful identities with concurrent operations.</p>
</section>
